{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791547810,"nonce":"SqeJTOTDCleyrsajFSh3lSL3L5Y=","title":"Typed ArrayIterator.next converts Float slot bits numerically","message":"At 94d14e941e8ad43a221f4996caa1ffe5639e60f8, the stdlib MIR ArrayIterator_next wrapper returns a raw I64 slot. Its ordinary method-call lowering leaves the result as I64 when ArrayIterator\u003cFloat\u003e.next is typed Float. The subsequent comparison emits an integer-to-Float numeric cast instead of reinterpreting the Float's bits. String results also remain integer slots rather than String pointers.\n\nUse the same slot-to-element conversion as Iterator\u003cT\u003e protocol handles for both stdlib wrapper call paths. Preserve boxed-slot tracking for Dynamic elements. DynamicArrayIteration checks direct typed Int, Float and String iterators alongside erased iteration. MIR evidence: /tmp/rayzor-dynamic-array-iteration-mir-v3.log, ArrayIterator_next call followed by cast i64 to f64. Native Haxe passes the fixture; Cranelift and LLVM both fail with typed float iterator in the v3 logs. Validation pending.","files":null}]}